  "content": {
    "id": "p44-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $L(x)=x+1$ and $Q(x)=x^2+2x$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p44-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "State the ranges of $L(x)$ and $Q(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$L$: all real $y$; $Q$: $y\\geq-1$.",
          "worked": "$L(x)=x+1$ takes every real value. Since $Q(x)=(x+1)^2-1$ and $(x+1)^2\\geq0$, the minimum of $Q$ is $-1$, attained at $x=-1$. Its range is $[-1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $L(Q(x))$ and determine its range.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$L(Q(x))=x^2+2x+1=(x+1)^2$; range $y\\geq0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}L(Q(x))&=(x^2+2x)+1\\\\L(Q(x))&=(x+1)^2\\end{align*}$$The minimum is $0$, attained at $x=-1$, so the range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find $Q(L(x))$ and determine its range.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q(L(x))=x^2+4x+3=(x+1)(x+3)$; range $y\\geq-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}Q(L(x))&=(x+1)^2+2(x+1)\\\\Q(L(x))&=x^2+4x+3\\\\Q(L(x))&=(x+2)^2-1\\end{align*}$$The minimum is $-1$, attained at $x=-2$, so the range is $[-1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find the zeroes of $Q(L(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-1$ and $-3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}Q(L(x))&=(x+1)(x+3)\\end{align*}$$A zero occurs when $x+1=0$ or $x+3=0$. Hence the zeroes are $x=-1$ and $x=-3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 13
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q18-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Show that $Q\\left(L\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)\\right)=\\frac{(x+2)(3x+4)}{(x+1)^2}$, provided that $x\\neq-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q\\left(L\\left(\\frac1{x+1}\\right)\\right)=\\frac{(x+2)(3x+4)}{(x+1)^2}$ for $x\\neq-1$.",
          "worked": "For $x\\neq-1$:$$\\begin{align*}L\\left(\\frac1{x+1}\\right)&=\\frac1{x+1}+1\\\\L\\left(\\frac1{x+1}\\right)&=\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\\\Q\\left(L\\left(\\frac1{x+1}\\right)\\right)&=\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)^2+2\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)\\\\Q\\left(L\\left(\\frac1{x+1}\\right)\\right)&=\\frac{(x+2)^2+2(x+2)(x+1)}{(x+1)^2}\\\\Q\\left(L\\left(\\frac1{x+1}\\right)\\right)&=\\frac{(x+2)(3x+4)}{(x+1)^2}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
